\ CODEHIGH.SEQ  Call high level code from assembly & return
\                                                       F-PC native port

comment:

  Allow calling high level words from within a CODE definition.

  WARNING ! You must not assume your registers are preserved across a
group of high level words within the CODE definition. They ARE NOT !
If you need to preserve some registers, then doit yourself.

  Native port: CODE words are abstract-machine code (AMASM.SEQ). >H lays
down ops that push the Forth IP on the return stack, point IP at a token
list and return to the inner interpreter (NEXT,), so the high level words
that follow are compiled into that list. H> ends the list with a token
that runs the rest of the CODE word: a nameless code field with its own
;CODE style code token, whose ops pop the saved IP back first. (The
original called HDOES, which nested into the list, and HRET jumped back.)

  Also: R0-R7 are not preserved (as before), the stacks are shared, and W
holds that nameless code field after H>, not the CODE word's own. Don't
branch to a local label on the other side of a >H ... H> group: each part
is a separate op stream to the JIT.

comment;

only forth also assembler definitions also

: >H            ( --- )         \ start high level code in a CODE word
                IP RPUSH,                       \ save the caller's IP
                XALIGN IP XHERE LI,             \ run the list that follows
                NEXT,
                also forth ] ;

: H>            ( --- )         \ back to the assembler
                previous
                HERE X,                         \ a code field that runs
                HERE CELL+ NEW-AMCODE ,         \ the ops that follow it
                [compile] [
                IP RPOP, ; immediate            \ restore the caller's IP

only forth also definitions

\s

Example         A simple example of the use of High level in a code word.

        CODE TST
                R0 23 LI,
                R0 PUSH,
                >H DUP . H>     \ you MUST come back with "H>".
                R0 POP,
                NEXT,
                END-CODE
